Input files
(it is driven by award_names + nominees_by_award + thresholds)
1.gg2013_preprocessed.jsonl fields like qt_text/rt_text/clean_text/text_no_tags/lang/lang_conf/timestamp_ms/is_quote/is_retweet.
2.awards_2013.txt (one official award name per line).
3.nominees_2013.json ({award: [nominees]}),!!! no winners inside!!!

i run the codes with the parameter below and gets 95% accuracy:
  --tweets ./gg2013_preprocessed.jsonl \
  --awards ./awards_2013.txt \
  --nominees ./nominees_2013.json \
  --out ./outputs/2013.results.json \
  --fast-person --langs "en,unk" \
  --min-anchor 2 --min-jaccard 0.35 --time-pct 0.7
votes, winners_map, topk = identify_winners(
    tweets, awards, nominees_by_award,
    person_extractor=None,          
    strict_nominees=False,          
    min_jaccard=0.35,             
    min_anchor=2,                
    time_pct=0.7                   
)
from winners import identify_winners → returns (votes, winners, topk)

Key params:
--fast-person (regex NER), --langs, --min-anchor, --min-jaccard, --time-pct. strict_nominees=False as our nominees don’t contain winners

Normalize title winners to the official nominees spelling before writing final JSON (map hashtag/concat variants to the longest matching nominee).
